%S A400974 1,2,0,3,0,4,0,4,1,0,0,6,0,0,2,5,0,6,0,6,0,0,0,8,1,0,0,6,0,8,0,6,0,0,
%T A400974 2,9,0,0,0,8,0,8,0,0,4,0,0,10,1,2,0,0,0,8,0,8,0,0,0,12,0,0,2,7,0,8,0,
%U A400974 0,0,4,0,12,0,0,0,0,2,0,0,10,1,0,0,12
%N A400974 Number of terms in the central 2-dense sublist of divisors of n, or 0 if such a sublist does not exist.
%C A400974 If n has an even number of 2-dense sublists of divisors then the central 2-dense sublist does no exist therefore a(n) = 0.
%C A400974 a(n) is the product of the ruler function of n and the number of odd terms in the central 2-dense sublist of divisors of n.
%H A400974 Paolo Xausa, <a href="/A400974/b400974.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%F A400974 Conjecture: a(n) = A001511(n) * A384230(n).
%e A400974 For n = 44 the divisors of 44 are 1, 2, 4, 11, 22, 44. There are two 2-dense sublists of divisors, they are [1, 2, 4] and [11, 22, 44]. The central sublist does not exist so a(44) = 0.
%e A400974 For n = 70 the divisors of 70 are 1, 2, 5, 7, 10, 14, 35, 70. There are three 2-dense sublists of divisors, they are [1, 2], [5, 7, 10, 14], [35, 70]. The central sublist is [5, 7, 10, 14] which has four terms, so a(70) = 4.
%t A400974 A400974[n_] := If[OddQ[Length[#]], Length[#[[(Length[#] + 1)/2]]], 0] & [Split[Divisors[n], #2 <= 2*# &]];
%t A400974 Array[A400974, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Nov 15 2026 *)
%Y A400974 Indices of zeros give A071561.
%Y A400974 Indices of positive terms give A071562.
%Y A400974 Cf. A001511, A067742, A174973 (2-dense numbers), A237270, A237271, A237593, A384222, A384230, A400194.
%K A400974 nonn,new
%O A400974 1,2
